WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.240
The example of the WHERE clause we have seen uses&nbsp;
precise criteria to filter records down to what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.000
want within a query. Sometimes though, we may not&nbsp;
know the exact spelling of the criteria for which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.800
we are looking. In these situations, a LIKE clause&nbsp;
added to a WHERE clause allows us to use wildcards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.640
to get approximate matches for criteria. For this&nbsp;
query, instead of using an exact match for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.400
city criteria, let's say we are not sure of the&nbsp;
city name but that it starts with the letters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:37.120
LO. I will adjust the City criterion to first&nbsp;
change the equals sign to the keyword LIKE.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:48.720
Then, I will change Los Angeles to LO%. The&nbsp;
percent symbol (%) is a wildcard that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.960
any number of characters. The criterion&nbsp;
now basically reads this way: The city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.480
must start with the letters LO but can have&nbsp;
anything after it. I will execute this query,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.480
and we see that we have about 600 records and&nbsp;
multiple cities that start with the letters LO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:16.000
Let's see what happens if we add a percent symbol&nbsp;
in front of the L and then execute this again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:25.280
We are now over 1500 records. The reason is that&nbsp;
the criteria now reads: We want to see any city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.640
with the letters LO in the name, no matter&nbsp;
what is in front of the LO or after the LO.

